{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-04T09:59:00.148Z","id":"msg_011CdhWdN6iTnNZkiGDEMinG","input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":843,"content":[{"text":"Docker's running and the first full container test already passed. Here's where things stand while the confirmation run completes.\n\n## First run: everything installed, exit 0\n\nVerified in a clean `ubuntu:24.04` container as an unprivileged `student` account with sudo — the same shape as a real WSL install:\n\n```\ngit 2.43.0    rg 14.1.0     jq 1.7        bat 0.24.0    fd 9.0.0\nclang 18.1.3  lldb 18.1.3   valgrind 3.22.0   xxd   hexyl 0.8.0   objdump 2.42\nuv 0.12.1     ruff 0.16.1   Python 3.14.6\njava 25.0.4 (Temurin LTS)   javac 25.0.4  Maven 3.8.7   Gradle 9.6.1\nJAVA_HOME=/usr/lib/jvm/temurin-25-jdk-arm64\n```\n\nThe Adoptium repo worked, `uv python install 3.14` resolved to 3.14.6, and Gradle's version API returned 9.6.1. The C installer's built-in smoke test compiled, ran, and dumped a real ELF header:\n\n```\n00000000: 7f45 4c46 0201 0100 ...  .ELF............\n```\n\n## One real bug found and fixed\n\nThe version summary printed `gradle` as blank and `maven` with raw ANSI codes (`[1mApache Maven 3.8.7[m`). Cause: `verify()` in `common.sh` did `head -n 1` — but `gradle --version` opens with a blank line, and `mvn -version` wraps its banner in bold escapes. Fixed to strip ANSI and take the first *non-blank* line. Cosmetic, but it's the summary users read to confirm the install worked, so a blank next to `gradle` reads as failure.\n\n`shellcheck -x -S style` still clean after the change.\n\n## One non-bug\n\nMy first harness reported C compile errors — that was my own shell-escaping mistake building the test file through nested quoting, not a script problem. The installer's own smoke test passed in the same run. Rewritten using heredocs.\n\n## In flight\n\nRunning `install_all.sh` **twice** in one container to confirm the `verify()` fix and prove the idempotency claim — checking that pass 2 skips rather than reinstalls, and that `~/.bashrc` still has exactly 3 markers rather than 6. I'll report when it lands.\n\nStill untested: `linux/amd64`, which is what your users actually run. Say the word after this and I'll do an emulated pass.","type":"text"}]}
