{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-04T10:52:36.838Z","id":"msg_011CdhaigoDmabbPdqHCF19R","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":588,"content":[{"text":"The amd64 run found something worth having: my sanitizer check was too strict.\n\n**What happened:** on emulated x86_64 the test binary was `Killed` (exit 137). ASan reserves a ~20TB shadow-memory mapping that Docker's x86-on-ARM emulation can't satisfy, so the program dies before producing any report. My check treated that as \"sanitizer broken\" and flagged the entire C install as failed — even though every tool installed correctly.\n\n**Why that mattered beyond this test:** the same false alarm would hit real users in memory-capped containers, some CI runners, and restricted environments — telling them their install broke when it didn't.\n\nNow the check distinguishes three cases:\n\n| Outcome | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| Won't link | Real failure — the runtime package is missing (the original bug) |\n| Runs, reports the overflow | Pass |\n| Killed (137/139) | Warn only — installed correctly, can't run *here*, works on real Ubuntu/WSL |\n\n**What amd64 did confirm:** `libclang-rt-18-dev:amd64` exists and installs cleanly, plus `clang`, `clang-format`, `clang-tidy`, `clang-tools`. That was the actual purpose of the amd64 pass — verifying package availability on your users' architecture — and it succeeded.\n\nTo be precise about the limits: I'm confident the SIGKILL is an emulation artifact (the failure mode is the binary never starting, not ASan finding nothing), and ASan on native x86_64 Linux is about as well-trodden as configurations get. But I haven't run it on native x86_64, so that's inference from the failure mode, not a direct observation.\n\nVerifying the amd64 path now behaves gracefully; I'll confirm when it finishes.","type":"text"}]}
