{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-04T12:59:17.263Z","id":"msg_011CdhkNgFqF5Z7YYTfUtjig","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":985,"content":[{"text":"Both restructurings are done. Verification running with `SYS_PTRACE` enabled so `lldb` and `pdb` can actually debug.\n\n## Step 4 → `python3` + pdb\n\nInstalls `python3` only. pdb needs nothing — it's in the standard library, which is a genuine advantage worth teaching: it works on any machine, including a server you just SSH'd into.\n\nTwo sample files written to `~/python-samples`, designed so tracing is meaningful:\n- **`stats.py`** — `mean`, `median`, `spread`\n- **`report.py`** — imports `stats`, and crashes on purpose with `ZeroDivisionError` on an empty list\n\nThat gives three distinct debugger lessons: stepping *into* another file (`s` vs `n`), post-mortem inspection via `python3 -m pdb -c continue`, and reading the call stack with `w`. The page also covers `breakpoint()` and `python3 -m trace` for whole-run tracing.\n\n## Step 5 → `javac` + `java` + `jshell`\n\nJDK only; Maven and Gradle removed. Two samples in `~/java-samples`:\n- **`Stats.java`** — static methods\n- **`Report.java`** — calls `Stats`, takes numbers from the command line\n\n`javac Report.java` compiles `Stats.java` too, which is a nice thing to notice. Also shows `java Report.java` (no compile step) and `/open Stats.java` in jshell.\n\nAdded a jshell smoke test to the installer — it evaluates an expression and checks the answer, so a broken jshell can't pass silently.\n\n## New independent steps\n\n**Step 8** — `install_uv.sh`, all the uv material, plus a new section on debugging uv projects (`uv run python -m pdb main.py`) that connects back to step 4.\n**Step 9** — `install_maven_gradle.sh`, with a Maven-vs-Gradle comparison and the \"adding a library\" section that explains *why* you'd want a build tool. It warns rather than fails if no JDK is present.\n\nI dropped the `jq` dependency from Gradle's version lookup — jq now lives in optional step 6, so step 9 falls back to `sed` parsing.\n\n## Also fixed\n\nThe earlier container run flagged `lldb did not stop at a breakpoint`. Investigated: Docker's default seccomp blocks ptrace (`personality set failed: Operation not permitted`); with `--cap-add=SYS_PTRACE` it works perfectly. Environment restriction, not a bug — now detected and reported as such, same as the ASan case.\n\nOne thing that isn't a leak despite looking like one: `objdump`, `nm` and `readelf` exist after step 3 because `binutils` is a dependency of `clang`. Unavoidable — clang needs an assembler and linker. Noted in CLAUDE.md so it isn't \"fixed\" later.\n\n`shellcheck -x -S style` clean, 128/128 links resolve. Results shortly.","type":"text"}]}
