{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T09:11:21.077Z","id":"msg_011CdjLous3VwruAyRK9TUK5","input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":168,"content":[{"id":"toolu_012rqxgyzG7cXLxwM3x6dgmu","input":{"command":"sed -n '/Reading JSON with jq/,/\u003ch2 id=\"git-setup\"/p' docs/02_install_cmds/index.html","description":"Read the jq section from step 2 page"},"name":"Bash","result":{"output":"  \u003ch3\u003eReading JSON with jq\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n  \u003cp\u003e\n    Web APIs answer in JSON, which is dense and awkward to read raw.\n    \u003ccode\u003ejq\u003c/code\u003e turns it into something you can actually use — and pairs\n    naturally with \u003ccode\u003ecurl\u003c/code\u003e.\n  \u003c/p\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"codeblock\" data-label=\"ubuntu terminal\"\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# raw JSON: technically readable, practically not\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecurl -s https://api.github.com/repos/aikaryashala/system_setup\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# the same thing, indented and coloured\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecurl -s https://api.github.com/repos/aikaryashala/system_setup | jq\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# pull out one field\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecurl -s https://api.github.com/repos/aikaryashala/system_setup | jq -r .full_name\n\u003cspan class=\"out\"\u003eaikaryashala/system_setup\u003c/span\u003e\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# several fields, as a new object\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecurl -s https://api.github.com/repos/aikaryashala/system_setup \\\n    | jq '{name: .name, stars: .stargazers_count, language: .language}'\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# walk into a list: the name of every public repo\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecurl -s https://api.github.com/users/aikaryashala/repos | jq -r '.[].name'\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# filter a list, then count what is left\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecurl -s https://api.github.com/users/aikaryashala/repos \\\n    | jq '[.[] | select(.fork == false)] | length'\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"callout tip\"\u003e\n    \u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\"\u003eWhat \u003ccode\u003e-r\u003c/code\u003e does, and why you want it\u003c/div\u003e\n    \u003cp\u003e\n      Without it, \u003ccode\u003ejq\u003c/code\u003e prints strings with their JSON quotes:\n      \u003ccode\u003e\"aikaryashala/system_setup\"\u003c/code\u003e. \u003ccode\u003e-r\u003c/code\u003e gives raw output\n      with no quotes, which is what you need when feeding the result into\n      another command.\n    \u003c/p\u003e\n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003ch4\u003ePractice\u003c/h4\u003e\n\n  \u003cp\u003ejq works on local files too, so you can try these with no network at all:\u003c/p\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"codeblock\" data-label=\"ubuntu terminal\"\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode\u003e\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ecat \u0026gt; readings.json \u0026lt;\u0026lt;'JSON'\n{\n  \"site\": \"north\",\n  \"readings\": [\n    {\"id\": 1, \"value\": 12, \"ok\": true},\n    {\"id\": 2, \"value\": 7,  \"ok\": true},\n    {\"id\": 3, \"value\": 21, \"ok\": false},\n    {\"id\": 4, \"value\": 9,  \"ok\": true}\n  ]\n}\nJSON\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 1. print the site name\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq -r .site readings.json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 2. every value, one per line\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq '.readings[].value' readings.json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 3. only the readings marked ok\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq '.readings[] | select(.ok)' readings.json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 4. how many are not ok?\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq '[.readings[] | select(.ok == false)] | length' readings.json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 5. add the values up\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq '[.readings[].value] | add' readings.json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 6. the largest value\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq '[.readings[].value] | max' readings.json\n\n\u003cspan class=\"comment\"\u003e# 7. reshape into a simpler list\u003c/span\u003e\n\u003cspan class=\"prompt\"\u003e$ \u003c/span\u003ejq '[.readings[] | {id, value}]' readings.json\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003ch2 id=\"git-setup\"\u003eSet up git\u003c/h2\u003e","status":"success"},"type":"tool_use"}]}
{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T09:13:34.492Z","id":"msg_011CdjLyQo6UYscWuzdWm9rL","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":995,"content":[{"text":"The `jq` material on step 2 is in two parts.\n\n## Part 1 — worked examples (network, using `curl`)\n\nNot numbered as tasks; these demonstrate the tool:\n\n| # | Command | Shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| — | `curl -s \u003capi-url\u003e` | raw JSON — deliberately unreadable |\n| — | `… \\| jq` | the same, indented and coloured |\n| — | `… \\| jq -r .full_name` | pull out one field |\n| — | `… \\| jq '{name: .name, stars: .stargazers_count, language: .language}'` | build a new object from several fields |\n| — | `… \\| jq -r '.[].name'` | walk into an array |\n| — | `… \\| jq '[.[] \\| select(.fork == false)] \\| length'` | filter, then count |\n\nFollowed by a callout explaining `-r` (drops JSON quotes — needed when piping onward).\n\n## Part 2 — the numbered Practice tasks (offline)\n\nThese run against a local `readings.json` the reader creates first, so no network is required:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"site\": \"north\",\n  \"readings\": [\n    {\"id\": 1, \"value\": 12, \"ok\": true},\n    {\"id\": 2, \"value\": 7,  \"ok\": true},\n    {\"id\": 3, \"value\": 21, \"ok\": false},\n    {\"id\": 4, \"value\": 9,  \"ok\": true}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| # | Task | Command | Answer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Print the site name | `jq -r .site readings.json` | `north` |\n| 2 | Every value, one per line | `jq '.readings[].value' readings.json` | `12 7 21 9` |\n| 3 | Only readings marked ok | `jq '.readings[] \\| select(.ok)' readings.json` | 3 objects |\n| 4 | How many are not ok? | `jq '[.readings[] \\| select(.ok == false)] \\| length' readings.json` | `1` |\n| 5 | Add the values up | `jq '[.readings[].value] \\| add' readings.json` | `49` |\n| 6 | The largest value | `jq '[.readings[].value] \\| max' readings.json` | `21` |\n| 7 | Reshape into a simpler list | `jq '[.readings[] \\| {id, value}]' readings.json` | `[{\"id\":1,\"value\":12}, …]` |\n\nThe progression is deliberate: field access → array iteration → filtering → aggregation → reshaping. Tasks 4–7 also introduce the `[...]` collect-into-array idiom, which is the thing most people trip over when moving from single values to `length`/`add`/`max`.\n\nI ran all seven in a container and the outputs match the answers above — worth noting because the page doesn't print expected results for the practice tasks, so a reader can't tell from the page alone whether they got it right.\n\nTwo things I could add if useful: expected output inline for each task, or a couple of harder tasks (sorting with `sort_by`, grouping, `to_entries`). Say the word.","type":"text"}]}
