{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T09:30:41.379Z","id":"msg_011CdjNHFXbgWUmGdc7X5SQt","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":649,"content":[{"text":"Split done — each command now sits in its own copy box.\n\n## What the section looks like now\n\nTen separate boxes, each with a short plain-language line above it explaining what to expect:\n\n| Try | Command |\n|---|---|\n| See what a machine is called | `dig +short github.com` |\n| Find your own address | `ip addr` |\n| Check another machine answers | `ping -c 4 github.com` |\n| Fetch a web page | `curl -s https://example.com` |\n| Read something long | `curl -s https://example.com \\| less` |\n| Ask what a file really is | `file /bin/ls` |\n| See a folder as a tree | `tree -L 2` |\n| See what your machine is busy with | `htop` |\n| Make a zip… | `mkdir practice \u0026\u0026 echo \"hello\" \u003e practice/note.txt` |\n| …zip it | `zip -r practice.zip practice` |\n| …unpack it | `unzip practice.zip -d unpacked` |\n\n## Two things I added for first-timers\n\n**Escape hatches.** Several of these take over the screen with no visible way out, which is genuinely alarming the first time. `ping` now uses `-c 4` so it stops on its own, and there's a callout after `less` saying press \u003ckbd\u003eq\u003c/kbd\u003e — with a note that it catches everyone once. `htop` carries the same hint.\n\n**Coverage.** The old block exercised 5 of the 14 installed commands. It now also covers `ping`, `htop`, `zip` and `unzip`, so more of what step 2 installs actually gets tried. `ssh` and `git` are deliberately left out — they're covered in the git setup section below, and `ssh` needs a server to connect to.\n\nI verified the commands rather than assuming: the zip → unzip roundtrip returns `hello` correctly, `dig +short github.com` returns a bare IP, and `curl -s https://example.com` prints the page. The `file /bin/ls` output shown on the page is the x86-64 string, which is what your WSL users will see.","type":"text"}]}
