{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T10:05:24.398Z","id":"msg_011CdjQvoPH2fPg3kV1731jf","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":713,"content":[{"text":"Done — an existing Ubuntu of any version now stops the install.\n\n## Logic\n\n| Situation | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| `Ubuntu-24.04` present | skip, use it |\n| Any other Ubuntu (e.g. `Ubuntu-22.04`) | **skip the download entirely**, use what's there |\n| No Ubuntu at all | install `Ubuntu-24.04` |\n\nThe key detail: `$active` tracks whichever Ubuntu the machine will actually use, and `wsl --set-default $active` uses that. Setting the default to `Ubuntu-24.04` when only 22.04 exists would fail, so the default follows what's really installed rather than what was requested.\n\nWhat a 22.04 user sees:\n\n```\n skip Ubuntu is already installed: Ubuntu-22.04\n skip Skipping the Ubuntu-24.04 download - your existing Ubuntu will be used.\n\n  These guides are written and tested against Ubuntu 24.04 LTS.\n  Almost everything works the same on Ubuntu-22.04, but if a package\n  name differs later, that is why.\n\n  To install 24.04 as well, run this yourself:\n    wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04\n```\n\nThey're told the guides target 24.04 and given the opt-in command, rather than either silently diverging or having 24.04 forced on them.\n\nThe final \"launch Ubuntu from the Start menu\" message now names `$active`, and only says *\"It appears as Ubuntu 24.04.x LTS\"* when that's actually what got installed.\n\n## Docs\n\nStep 1's page callout rewritten (it previously said 24.04 installs alongside — now wrong), a dedicated \"If you already have Ubuntu 22.04\" callout with the opt-in command, the what-it-does list updated, and the verification section reworded to talk about *your* Ubuntu rather than assuming the name. README and CLAUDE.md note the skip behaviour.\n\n141/141 links resolve. Still unexecuted — this is PowerShell and needs real Windows, so it remains reviewed and linted only.\n\nStep 3's beginner rewrite is still pending whenever you want it.","type":"text"}]}
