{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T10:23:13.793Z","id":"msg_011CdjSHEczhvQv1yHyCFdmn","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1002,"content":[{"text":"It's four separate pieces glued together. Reading left to right:\n\n```powershell\n\u0026 wsl.exe --list --verbose 2\u003e\u00261 | Out-Host\n│ │        │                │      │\n│ │        │                │      └── show it on screen, don't return it\n│ │        │                └───────── merge the error stream into the output stream\n│ │        └────────────────────────── arguments: list distros, with detail\n│ └─────────────────────────────────── the program to run\n└───────────────────────────────────── the call operator\n```\n\n## `\u0026` — the call operator\n\nTells PowerShell \"run this as a command.\" Here it's **optional** — `wsl.exe --list --verbose` alone would work identically, since `wsl.exe` is a literal name. It's required when the command name lives in a variable (`\u0026 $exe --list`). I used it consistently on every external call so they're all visibly \"this is an outside program, not a PowerShell cmdlet.\"\n\nWriting `wsl.exe` rather than `wsl` is also deliberate: it can't be shadowed by a PowerShell alias or function someone has defined.\n\n## `2\u003e\u00261` — merge stderr into stdout\n\n`2` is the error stream, `1` is the output stream. This says \"put errors in with the normal output.\"\n\nTwo reasons it matters here:\n\n1. `wsl.exe` writes some ordinary progress chatter to stderr, not just real errors. Without merging, PowerShell renders those as **red error text**, which looks alarming to a first-time user when nothing is wrong.\n2. Line 27 sets `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'`. When a native program writes to stderr, PowerShell can wrap that in a `NativeCommandError` record — and under `Stop`, that can abort the whole script. Merging the streams turns it into plain text instead of something that can kill the run.\n\n## `| Out-Host` — print it, don't return it\n\nThis is the subtlest part, and the reason it isn't just left off.\n\nIn PowerShell, **anything a function emits that nobody consumes becomes part of its return value.** This line sits inside the `Install-UbuntuWsl` function. Without `Out-Host`, the distro table would silently become the function's output — so a future `$result = Install-UbuntuWsl` would get a jumble of wsl.exe text instead of what you expected.\n\n`Out-Host` writes straight to the console and returns nothing to the pipeline. Meaning: *\"this is for the human reading the screen, not data for the program.\"*\n\n## Related\n\nLine 31's `$env:WSL_UTF8 = '1'` is the same family of problem. `wsl.exe` emits UTF-16 by default, which PowerShell renders as text riddled with null characters — `U.b.u.n.t.u`. That variable makes it emit normal UTF-8 so the table above is readable at all.\n\n## In context\n\nLine 212 is the closing summary — after everything is installed, it shows the user what they ended up with:\n\n```\nInstalled\n------------------------------------------------------------\n  NAME            STATE           VERSION\n* Ubuntu-24.04    Stopped         2\n```","type":"text"}]}
