{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T10:30:00.834Z","id":"msg_011CdjSoRRTzgiBtzh8Qo9Mp","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":822,"content":[{"text":"Fixed. Two distinct bugs, both mine:\n\n## Bug 1 — `wsl --update --no-launch`\n\n`--no-launch` is only ever an option for `wsl --install`. Passing it to `--update` makes WSL reject the entire command line — which is the error you saw. Removed, with a comment so it doesn't get re-added:\n\n```powershell\n# --update takes no other options. Do not add --no-launch here: it is only\n# understood by --install, and WSL rejects the whole command.\n\u0026 wsl.exe --update 2\u003e\u00261 | Out-Host\n```\n\nMy old fallback did eventually retry without the flag, so the update still happened — but you saw an alarming error message first, in a script meant for people who've never opened a terminal.\n\n## Bug 2 — the same flag on `--install`, unguarded\n\nHere `--no-launch` is legitimate, but it only exists on the **Microsoft Store** build of WSL. The inbox WSL bundled with Windows rejects it — and my code read that rejection as \"the install failed\", bailed out, and told the user to install manually. On those machines step 1 would simply never have worked.\n\nNow it asks first:\n\n```powershell\nif (Test-WslNoLaunchSupport) {\n    \u0026 wsl.exe --install --no-launch -d $distro 2\u003e\u00261 | Out-Host\n} else {\n    Write-Skip 'This WSL build has no --no-launch option; installing without it.'\n    Write-Skip 'Ubuntu may open its own window and ask for a username - that is fine.'\n    \u0026 wsl.exe --install -d $distro 2\u003e\u00261 | Out-Host\n}\n```\n\nThe probe uses `wsl --version`, which succeeds only on the Store build — the same build that introduced `--no-launch`. That's a real signal rather than parsing help text, which is localised and shifts between releases.\n\nThe `else` branch also warns that Ubuntu may pop open its own setup window, since without `--no-launch` it launches immediately. Better to predict it than let it surprise them.\n\n## Caveat\n\nStill unverified by execution — I have no Windows and no PowerShell here, so this is reasoning from WSL's documented behaviour and your error message, not from a run. If anything else in step 1 misbehaves, send the message and I'll fix it the same way.\n\nThat's now **three** bugs found in this script by you running it, versus zero found by review and linting. Worth noting for the record: PSScriptAnalyzer would never have caught any of them — they're all wrong arguments to an external program, which no linter checks.","type":"text"}]}
