{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T10:54:43.763Z","id":"msg_011CdjUgn11RgGyEVFJ69CB9","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":822,"content":[{"text":"You were right — step 4 never ran the files it hands the student.\n\n## What was and wasn't tested\n\nThe script *did* run Python, but only on a throwaway file it wrote to `/tmp`. The two samples placed in `~/python-samples` — the ones a student actually types against — were written and never executed. A typo inside those heredocs would have shipped, and the student would have no way to tell a broken sample from their own mistake.\n\n`check_samples()` now runs them for real:\n\n| Check | Proves |\n|---|---|\n| `report.py` prints `--- readings ---` and `median: 10.5` | `import stats` resolved and the arithmetic ran |\n| Output contains `ZeroDivisionError` | the deliberate bug is still there to practise on |\n| pdb post-mortem prints `PM=[]` | the whole lesson on the page works on this machine |\n\nIt runs from the samples directory, since `import stats` only resolves when both files are in the working directory — and it judges by **output, not exit code**, because `report.py` exits 1 on purpose.\n\n## A fabricated output, corrected\n\nRunning the samples caught something worse than the missing test. The page showed this:\n\n```\n--- missing ---\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"report.py\", line 24, in \u003cmodule\u003e\n```\n\n**`--- missing ---` never prints.** `show()` calls `stats.summarise()` on its first line, so it dies before reaching the `print`. I'd written what looked plausible instead of what the program does — and the line number was wrong too.\n\nThat absence turned out to be worth teaching, so it's now a callout: the missing heading is the first clue about *where* the failure happened, and the traceback's last frame names `stats.py`, not `report.py`. Reading output for what isn't there is a genuine debugging skill.\n\n## Also fixed on the page\n\n\"Check it worked\" was three commands that only asked *is it installed?* — versions and an import. Now each is its own copy box, plus the one that actually proves Python works: running `report.py` and seeing both the numbers and the error.\n\n## Two caveats\n\n- I verified this with local Python 3.13; Docker is stopped, so I haven't re-run the installer end to end on Ubuntu 3.12. The grep patterns match the real output I captured.\n- `docs/examples/report.py` and the copy embedded in `install_py.sh` have **different docstrings**, so their line numbers differ — CLAUDE.md says they should be identical. Worth syncing; say the word and I'll generate the heredocs from the example files so they can't drift.","type":"text"}]}
