act\n\nEvery `.sh` file in `docs/scripts/` must:\n\n1. Start with `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and `set -euo pipefail`.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"## Script contract\n\nEvery `.sh` file in `docs/scripts/` must:\n\n1. Start with `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and `set -euo pipefail`. The one exception is\n   `report_completion.sh`, which uses `set -uo pipefail`: it has to survive a\n   failing check in order to report it, which is the whole point of that script.\"\"\")\nt = t.replace(\"\"\"## Tone of the website copy\"\"\",\n\"\"\"## report_completion.sh\n\nStep 5.5 reports a student's progress to a Google Form. Three things about it\nare load-bearing:\n\n- **It reads from `/dev/tty`, never stdin.** The documented way to run it is\n  `curl … | bash`, where stdin is the script itself — a plain `read` would\n  swallow script text instead of waiting for the student.\n- **It tests by doing, not by looking.** `command -v clang` proves nothing; the\n  script compiles and runs a program. A tool that exists but cannot work is a\n  failure, and gets named in the report.\n- **It reports failures too**, with what broke, so a teacher can see where a\n  student is stuck. Never change it to only submit on success.\n\n`DRY_RUN=1` runs every check and sends nothing. Use it for any testing — do not\nsubmit test rows to the live form.\n\n## Tone of the website copy\"\"\")\np.write_text(t)\nprint(\"wired in\")\nPY\n./tools/check_links.sh docs \u0026\u0026 shellcheck -x -S style docs/scripts/*.sh tools/*.sh \u0026\u0026 echo \"=== ALL CHECKS CLEAN ===\"","description":"Wire step 5.5 into landing, scripts index, README and CLAUDE.md"},"name":"Bash","result":{"output":"wired in\nAll 171 local links resolve.\n=== ALL CHECKS CLEAN ===","status":"success"},"type":"tool_use"}]}
{"v":1,"agent":"claude-code","cli_version":"0.9.0","type":"assistant","ts":"2026-08-05T11:46:13.689Z","id":"msg_011CdjYceo2HbBsYwGnKmWjS","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":772,"content":[{"text":"Step 5.5 is in — `docs/scripts/report_completion.sh` plus a page at `docs/05_5_report/`.\n\n## One thing in your sample would have broken\n\n```bash\nread -rp \"Student Name : \" NAME\n```\n\nWith `curl … | bash` — the way every other step is run — **stdin is the script itself**, so the prompts consume script text instead of waiting for the student. Now reads from `/dev/tty`, with a clear error if there's no terminal, and `STUDENT_NAME`/`ROLL_NUMBER` env vars as a non-interactive path.\n\nYour form URL, all four `entry.*` IDs, `HASH=\"AIKSUBMIT\"`, and the HTTP-200 check are used exactly as given.\n\n## It tests by doing, not by looking\n\n`command -v clang` proves nothing — a tool can exist and still be unable to work. So it:\n\n| Step | Test |\n|---|---|\n| 2 | all 15 commands present, then `jq` validates a JSON file |\n| 3 | `clang` compiles + runs a program; `lldb` stops at a breakpoint |\n| 4 | `python3` runs a script; `pdb` breaks and reads a variable |\n| 5 | `javac` compiles, `java` runs it, `jshell` evaluates an expression |\n\n`set -uo pipefail`, **not `-e`** — it must survive a failing check in order to report it.\n\n## Status\n\nPassing → `Completed`. Otherwise the failures are named:\n\n```\nFailed: step2 missing: ip tree htop, step3 lldb cannot debug, step5 javac cannot compile\n```\n\nCapped at 300 chars for the form field. One nuance: if `lldb` can't debug because the environment forbids ptrace (containers), that's reported as `skip`, not a failure — it isn't the student's fault.\n\n## Tested\n\nRan it on this Mac, which is missing `ip`/`tree`/`htop` and has no working JDK — so the failure path got a real exercise, and produced exactly the status line above.\n\n**I used `DRY_RUN=1` throughout and submitted nothing to your form.** Sending test rows to a live form your students use isn't mine to do. When you want a real end-to-end check, run it yourself, or tell me to and I will.\n\n`shellcheck -x -S style` clean, 171/171 links resolve.","type":"text"}]}
